steps.body:
Two keywords change a loop's flow from inside the body.

- `continue` skips the rest of this pass. The post statement still runs, then the condition is checked again.
- `break` leaves the loop right away. The program carries on after the loop's closing brace.

```go
for i := 1; i <= 100; i++ {
	if i > 4 {
		break // stop the whole loop
	}
	if i%2 == 0 {
		continue // skip the print for even i
	}
	fmt.Println(i) // 1, 3
}
```

Because `continue` still runs `i++`, a three-part loop never skips its counter update. Use `break` when the condition in the header is not the only way to finish, such as stopping early once you have seen enough.

Both affect only the innermost `for` that contains them. One gotcha: inside a `switch` within a loop, `break` leaves the `switch`, not the loop. Use an `if` when you need to break out of the loop. `continue` has no such trap: inside a `switch` it still moves the loop to its next pass.

